BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Art Exhibit: Endless Inspiration
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261117T110000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261117T180000
UID:261014KqCF1kbH4k-RPPlwjimCHQ
DESCRIPTION:Date: FOR IMMEDIATE RELEASE  10/12/26\n\nNOVEMBER EXHIBIT: EN
 DLESS INSPIRATION\n\nEXHIBIT DATES – October 26  – November 22\, 2026
 \n\nRECEPTION: SATURDAY\, November 7\, 2026\, 3-6 pm: Join us to meet Ron 
 Sumner and Karen Miller and learn about their art and inspiration.  Be su
 re to take part in the Gallery’s free monthlong drawing for a beautiful 
 work by a Gallery artist.  Refreshments will be served.\n\nSonoma County 
 artist\, Ron Sumner\, presents Endless Inspiration\, a collection of new p
 aintings and classic works that highlight his lifelong love of nature feat
 uring landscapes\, animals and wildlife\, with a special focus on birds. 
  The essence of this golden state shines forth in “California Hills\,”
  a large oil painting of a sweep of oak covered hills.   Turning to wild
 life\, Sumner’s masterful watercolor rendition in the “Young Fox” ca
 ptures its wild nature on stark alert.  And the compelling presence of a 
 most majestic bird is beautifully memorialized in his “Blue Heron” wat
 ercolor.  Other birds depicted in his works include parrots\, penguins an
 d owls.  “In my painting\, I always strive to translate my emotional re
 sponse to the world of nature around us – to share this deep feeling wit
 h those who view my work\,” commented Sumner on his inspiration.\n\nA li
 felong Northern Californian\, Sumner began his art training in school.  A
 fter earning a BFA from the California College of Arts & Crafts\, he was a
  technical illustrator for a number of years before transitioning to a ful
 l-time fine arts painter.  With an emphasis in watercolor\, he has won nu
 merous awards\, including Best of Show at the Society of Western Artists.
   His portrait of John F. Kennedy is part of the permanent collection at 
 the John F. Kennedy Library.  He has exhibited widely and was part of the
  Veterans Art Show for CA vets in 2023.\n\nMeet the artist: Sumner will co
 -host Art Nite on 11/5 from 5 pm to 7 pm.\n\nThe Stairway Smallworks Showc
 ase features Karen Miller’s Historic Gems\, featuring pastel paintings t
 hat honor our history – local artifacts that are disappearing.  Highlig
 hting this exhibit\, “Ghost Bridge\,” an early 20th century railroad b
 ridge known as Healdsburg Railroad Bridge is soon to give way for the Smar
 t Train.  Its unique style is a key element to our local character but sa
 dly could not be upgraded for modern rail.  From old trucks to a beloved 
 schoolhouse\, these paintings harken fond memories.  Part of our world fo
 r years\, they enriched our lives and are not to be forgotten.  Noted Mil
 ler on the impetus for her series\, “I want to catch old things before t
 hey’re gone – memorializing those things close to our hearts.”\n\nMe
 et the artist: Miller will host the Gallery on October 26 and November 2\,
  11\, 14 & 15 from 11am to 2:30 pm.\n\n Also join us for ART NITE HEALDSB
 URG\, Thursday\, November 5\, 5 – 7 pm.  ART NITE Healdsburg\, held the
  1st Thursday of Every Month\, is a community event celebrating art and cr
 eativity with over 20 galleries participating.  ArtNiteHealdsburg.com.\n\
 nUpstairs Art Gallery\, 306 Center Street\, Healdsburg\, CA 95448. \\(707\
 \) 431-4214.: OPEN DAILY: 11am – 6 pm.    www.upstairsart gallery.net
  \n\nFor further information: Contact: Beverly Bird\, Press Contact\, at b
 vdrio@sonic.net or 707-799-1490.
LOCATION:Upstairs Art Gallery
URL:http://www.upstairsartgallery.net
X-SOURCE:Press Democrat
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Art Exhibit:  Endless Inspiration
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261117T110000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261117T180000
UID:261013yvoJUi6F0k2mYkG6kLx_ww
DESCRIPTION:Date: FOR IMMEDIATE RELEASE  10/12/26\n\n**NOVEMBER EXHIBIT: 
 ENDLESS INSPIRATION**\n\nEXHIBIT DATES **– October 26  – November 22\
 , 2026**\n\n**RECEPTION: SATURDAY\, November 7\, 2026\, 3-6 pm: Join us to
  meet Ron Sumner and Karen Miller and learn about their art and inspiratio
 n.  Be sure to take part in the Gallery’s free monthlong drawing for a 
 beautiful work by a Gallery artist.  Refreshments will be served.**\n\nSo
 noma County artist\, **Ron Sumner\,** presents **Endless Inspiration**\, a
  collection of new paintings and classic works that highlight his lifelong
  love of nature featuring landscapes\, animals and wildlife\, with a speci
 al focus on birds.  The essence of this golden state shines forth in “C
 alifornia Hills\,” a large oil painting of a sweep of oak covered hills.
    Turning to wildlife\, Sumner’s masterful watercolor rendition in th
 e “Young Fox” captures its wild nature on stark alert.  And the compe
 lling presence of a most majestic bird is beautifully memorialized in his 
 “Blue Heron” watercolor.  Other birds depicted in his works include p
 arrots\, penguins and owls.  “In my painting\, I always strive to trans
 late my emotional response to the world of nature around us – to share t
 his deep feeling with those who view my work\,” commented Sumner on his 
 inspiration.\n\nA lifelong Northern Californian\, Sumner began his art tra
 ining in school.  After earning a BFA from the California College of Arts
  & Crafts\, he was a technical illustrator for a number of years before tr
 ansitioning to a full-time fine arts painter.  With an emphasis in waterc
 olor\, he has won numerous awards\, including Best of Show at the Society 
 of Western Artists.  His portrait of John F. Kennedy is part of the perma
 nent collection at the John F. Kennedy Library.  He has exhibited widely 
 and was part of the Veterans Art Show for CA vets in 2023.\n\nMeet the art
 ist: Sumner will co-host Art Nite on 11/5 from 5 pm to 7 pm.\n\nThe Stairw
 ay Smallworks Showcase features **Karen Miller’**s **Historic Gems\,** f
 eaturing pastel paintings that honor our history – local artifacts that 
 are disappearing.  Highlighting this exhibit\, “Ghost Bridge\,” an ea
 rly 20th century railroad bridge known as Healdsburg Railroad Bridge is so
 on to give way for the Smart Train.  Its unique style is a key element to
  our local character but sadly could not be upgraded for modern rail.  Fr
 om old trucks to a beloved schoolhouse\, these paintings harken fond memor
 ies.  Part of our world for years\, they enriched our lives and are not t
 o be forgotten.  Noted Miller on the impetus for her series\, “I want t
 o catch old things before they’re gone – memorializing those things cl
 ose to our hearts.”\n\nMeet the artist: Miller will host the Gallery on 
 October 26 and November 2\, 11\, 14 & 15 from 11am to 2:30 pm.\n\n Also j
 oin us for **ART NITE HEALDSBURG**\, Thursday\, November 5\, 5 – 7 pm. 
  ART NITE Healdsburg\, held the 1st Thursday of Every Month\, is a communi
 ty event celebrating art and creativity with over 20 galleries participati
 ng.  ArtNiteHealdsburg.com.\n\nUpstairs Art Gallery\, 306 Center Street\,
  Healdsburg\, CA 95448. (707) 431-4214.: OPEN DAILY: 11am – 6 pm.    
 **www.upstairsart gallery.net** \n\nFor further information: Contact: Beve
 rly Bird\, Press Contact\, at bvdrio@sonic.net or 707-799-1490.
LOCATION:Upstairs Art Gallery
URL:http://www.upstairsartgallery.net
X-SOURCE:Press Democrat
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Freestyle Playground Dance Class
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261117T183000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261117T193000
UID:26100812MzCS8rPUqhi-TkVNItHg
DESCRIPTION:Freestyle Playground — 4-Week Dance & Movement Series\n\n**E
 xplore. Play. Create. Express.**\n\nReady to discover new ways of moving a
 nd expressing yourself through dance?\n\n**Freestyle Playground** is a pla
 yful\, creative dance class designed to expand your movement vocabulary\, 
 spark your imagination\, and help you discover your own unique movement st
 yle.\n\nThrough guided freestyle exercises\, imaginative movement concepts
 \, musicality\, elements of popping\, rhythm\, and body awareness\, you'll
  explore new ways to move\, experiment\, and create.\n\nEach week\, we'll 
 introduce different movement tools and concepts that you can combine\, bui
 ld upon\, and incorporate into your own dancing.\n\n**This isn't a choreog
 raphy-based class.** It's about exploration\, curiosity\, and the freedom 
 to move without worrying about getting it "right."\n\nWhether you're an ex
 perienced dancer looking for fresh inspiration or someone curious about ex
 ploring movement\, all levels are welcome!\n\nClass Details\n\n📍 **Lagu
 na Lab | Sebastopol\, CA**\n\n📅 **4 Tuesdays: October 27\, November 3\,
  10 & 17**\n\n⏰ **6:30–7:30 PM**\n\n💃 **All levels welcome!**\n\nCo
 me expand your creativity\, discover new movement possibilities\, and expe
 rience the joy of freestyle dance!\n\n**No perfection. Just movement\, mus
 ic\, imagination & PLAY!**
LOCATION:Laguna Lab
URL:https://www.tickettailor.com/events/lagunalab/2417443
X-SOURCE:Press Democrat
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Bottega Cellar Series with Stag's Leap Wine Cellars
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261117T180000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261117T210000
UID:26100697EhnSj5Z06zJzWWTcRmEw
DESCRIPTION:Join us at Bottega on November 17 for an elegant evening featu
 ring Stag’s Leap Wine Cellars\, hosted by Winemaker Marcus Notaro. Stag'
 s Leap Wine Cellars is a legendary Napa Valley estate established by Warre
 n Winiarski in 1970 in the Stags Leap District. Just six years after its f
 ounding\, its 1973 S.L.V. Cabernet Sauvignon stunned the world by besting 
 top Bordeaux wines\, including two First-Growths\, at the 1976 Judgment of
  Paris\, a victory that put California and Napa Valley on the global winem
 aking map. Join longtime winemaker Marcus Notaro as the estate continues i
 ts year-long 50th anniversary celebration of the Judgment of Paris. Guests
  will experience a thoughtfully curated progression of wines showcasing th
 e depth and character of the estate\, from vibrant whites to distinguished
  Napa Valley Cabernet Sauvignon\, paired with a seasonal multi-course menu
  by Bottega Executive Chef Alex Espinoza. Each course is designed to compl
 ement the character and expression of the wines\, bringing together refine
 d flavors\, exceptional ingredients\, and the gracious hospitality that de
 fines Bottega.\n\nPrice is inclusive of tax and gratuity.\n\n6525 Washingt
 on Street Suite A9\, Yountville\, CA 94599\n\n\\(707\\) 945-1050
LOCATION:Bottega Napa Valley
URL:https://www.opentable.com/booking/experiences-availability?rid=25489&r
 estref=25489&experienceId=791062&utm_source=external&utm_medium=referral&u
 tm_campaign=shared
X-SOURCE:Press Democrat
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:The Kabbalah of Meaning
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261117T180000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261117T193000
UID:261001rKW4rorDykWi0_6lb8dfyg
DESCRIPTION:Jewish Wisdom for a Life That Matters\n\nNo matter how much we
  achieve\, something within us still asks: “Is this it? Isn’t there so
 mething more?”\n\nThe Kabbalah of Meaning is a new six-session JLI cours
 e exploring the Jewish answer to that question. What are we really searchi
 ng for—and where can it be found?\n\nDrawing on Kabbalah and centuries o
 f Jewish wisdom\, we’ll uncover the essential building blocks of a deepl
 y meaningful life. We’ll explore how purpose can be found in our work\, 
 relationships\, challenges\, accomplishments\, and even the ordinary routi
 nes that fill our days.\n\nYou don’t have to revamp your life. You don
 ’t need to meditate on a mountaintop. You simply need a new lens through
  which to see the rich world of meaning already woven into every part of y
 our life.\n\nThoughtful\, practical\, and surprisingly relevant\, this cou
 rse offers fresh perspectives and useful tools for living with greater cla
 rity\, connection\, and intention.\n\nTry 1st class free.\n\nNo previous J
 ewish learning required. Come as you are—and bring your questions.
LOCATION:Chabad Jewish Center of Petaluma
URL:https://www.jewishpetaluma.com/templates/articlecco_cdo/aid/7522546/je
 wish/The-Kabbalah-of-Meaning.htm
X-SOURCE:Press Democrat
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Art Exhibit: Aspirations
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261117T110000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261117T180000
UID:2609167VNcrOjpikmARTLv33Rj1w
DESCRIPTION:Date: FOR IMMEDIATE RELEASE  9/15/26\n\n**OCTOBER EXHIBIT: AS
 PIRATIONS**\n\nEXHIBIT DATES **– September 28 – October 25\, 2026**\n\
 n**RECEPTION: SATURDAY\, October 3\, 2026\, 3-6 pm: Join us to meet Laura 
 Roney and Linda Loveland Reid and learn about their art and inspiration. 
  Be sure to take part in the Gallery’s free monthlong drawing for a beau
 tiful work by a Gallery artist.  Refreshments will be served.**\n\nSonoma
  County artist\, **Laura Roney\,** unveils **Aspirations**\, a new collect
 ion of paintings in oil\, celebrating the unique differing goals people se
 t during our lives\, challenging us to take bold steps forward.  Of unive
 rsal inspiration**\, Aspiration and Awe** splashes the Milky Way’s starr
 y brilliance across the heavens\, capturing our imagination and hope as wo
 rldly cares are left behind.  Moving from natural forces to human endeavo
 r\, looking toward social change\, **Aspiring to Vote**\, the portrait of 
 a hopeful suffragette\, evokes the dream for a better future.  A lifetime
  goal for many\, the marathon\, **Aspiring for 26.2\,** captures the early
  morning excitement waiting for start times.  “People doing ordinary th
 ings but dreaming larger for the future has been a great inspiration for t
 his series.  I hope folks will be inspired by the boldness of people’s 
 dreams\,” commented Roney on her inspiration for the work.\n\nArt has be
 en a lifelong passion for Roney.  Beginning her art studies at a young ag
 e\, whatever avenue she was pursuing in life\, she continued her artistic 
 endeavors.  After a successful career in microbiology and pharmaceutical 
 research\, a family move to Northern California enabled Roney to focus on 
 her art.  She received her MFA from Academy of Art University\, San Franc
 isco.\n\nMeet the artist: Roney will host the Gallery on September 30 and 
 October 1 from 11am to 6 pm.\n\nThe Stairway Smallworks Showcase features 
 Linda Loveland Reid’s **Intentional Chaos\,** a collection of exciting m
 ixed-media works that explode with excitement and energy. Random patterns\
 , amorphous shapes and dancing dribbles magically coalesce into bold state
 ments.  Intriguing and compelling\, like interlocking gears\, in **Patter
 ns of Memory** multiple patterns interlock on fields of gold and black.  
 “I love throwing ink\, paint and even coffee onto a blank canvas and cre
 ating through instinct\, gesture and emotion.  Distilling energy into sma
 ller works brings new challenges and some happy surprises\,” noted Reid 
 on her creative process.\n\nMeet the artist: Reid will host the Gallery on
  October 23 from 11am to 6 pm.\n\n Also join us for **ART NITE HEALDSBURG
 **\, Thursday\, October 1\, 5 – 7 pm.  ART NITE Healdsburg\, held the 1
 st Thursday of Every Month\, is a community event celebrating art and crea
 tivity with over 20 galleries participating.  ArtNiteHealdsburg.com.\n\nU
 pstairs Art Gallery\, 306 Center Street\, Healdsburg\, CA 95448. (707) 431
 -4214.: OPEN DAILY: 11am – 6 pm.    **www.upstairsart gallery.net** \
 n\nFor further information: Contact: Beverly Bird\, Press Contact\, at bvd
 rio@sonic.net or 707-799-1490.
LOCATION:Upstairs Art Gallery
URL:http://www.upstairsartgallery.net
X-SOURCE:Press Democrat
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Antarctica: The Frozen Desert | Photographs by Vegar Abelsnes
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261117T080000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261117T170000
UID:2609148ixR4Xf8qUSufsthyUDHDg
DESCRIPTION:1st Street Gallery at WTRSHD will present *“Antarctica: The 
 Frozen Desert\,”* a new body of fine art photography by Vegar Abelsnes\,
  opening September 26th\, 2026\, and on view September through November. 
  The exhibition marks a deliberate turn in Abelsnes’ photography\; after
  more than two decades of working in a commercial and advertising capacity
 \, he has embarked on a career as a photo and field guide in the polar reg
 ions. The exhibit features work generated over 6 *voyages to the* Antarcti
 c Peninsula. He is returning to Antarctica in January and will add the Fal
 klands and South Georgia to his list of destinations to guide and photogra
 ph.\n\nTraveling to some of the most remote and inhospitable coastlines on
  Earth\, Abelsnes has photographed a landscape that exists almost entirely
  outside of human time. The ice in these images began forming millions of 
 years ago\, while Homo sapiens have only existed for roughly 300\,000 year
 s. Defined by such a scale\, Antarctica is less a place than a record — 
 vast\, indifferent\, treacherous\, and staggeringly beautiful\, holding a 
 kind of cosmic truth that Abelsnes says is more important now than ever be
 fore.
LOCATION:1st Street Gallery @ WTRSHD
URL:https://vegarabelsnes.com/ANTARCTICA:-The-Frozen-Desert/thumbs
X-SOURCE:Press Democrat
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:California Native Creations Art Show
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261117T083000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261117T163000
UID:260909UuEv9sgLQUqI3nV6oGC3gg
DESCRIPTION:**September is the 17th Annual Pomo Honoring Month.** On Monda
 y\, Sept. 14\, the 6th California Native Creations Art Show opens Person S
 enior Wing\, Finley Community Center\, 2060 W. College Ave.\, Santa Rosa\,
  CA. The show is free and open to the public Monday – Friday\, 8:30 a.m.
 -4:30 p.m.\, through November 19.\n\nThe established and up-and-coming nat
 ive artists create with digital media\, paint and traditional materials in
  their art\, weaving\, regalia\, beadwork\, jewelry\, baskets and quilts.
  The artists include:\n\n·         Meyo Marrufo\, painter\, gues
 t curator for the de Young Museum and the Sonoma Valley Art Museum\,\n\n·
          Buffie Campbell\, Vice Chair\, Sherwood Valley Rancheria\
 , cultural teacher and master basket maker\,\n\n·         Henry E
 dward Frank\, *Yurok/Kashaya Pomo*\, who will show paintings and work with
  Legos titled *Mind Prison\,*\n\n·         Digital artist Nicolas
  (Nico) Vargas\, *Kashia Band of Pomo Indians*\, whose art was featured at
  the 55th Annual Stanford Powwow.\n\n·         plus Gloria Carret
 ero\; Rose Hammock\; Rudy Hammock\; Jocelyn Garcia\; Arella Ortega LaRose\
 , *Kashia Band of Pomo Indians*\; Angelica Smith\, *Manchester Point Arena
  and Yokayo Rancheria*\; Maria Pasalo\; Michelle Napoli\; Martina Morgan\,
  *Kashia Band of Pomo Indians*\; Hands Lopez\; Shashoni Hanover-Redenbaug
 h\; Katie Williams-Elliott\, Juan Partida\, and Mia Pasalo\, *Big Valley P
 omo Tribe*.
LOCATION:Person Senior Wing\, Finley Community Center
URL:http://www.pomoproject.org/
X-SOURCE:Press Democrat
END:VEVENT
